# First node retirement (2026-08-08). Pattern C1: delete the .mdx, 301 the
# live URL, repoint every in-corpus link. AICV measures dead URLs as a defect
# in other sites; shipping one of our own is not available to us.
#
# WRITE BOTH FORMS. These rules are PATH-EXACT. This site's canonical node URL
# is the TRAILING-SLASH form (/nodes/x -> 308 -> /nodes/x/), so the slash rule
# is the one real traffic hits; the bare form is the belt-and-braces. Shipping
# only the bare rule on 2026-08-08 left the retired page live at its canonical
# URL, served from a ~23h stale edge entry with the very DCF text the deploy
# had just corrected. A redirect rule is evaluated BEFORE asset serving, so the
# slash rule also masks a stale edge entry instead of waiting it out.
/nodes/sunshinefm-startup-studios/ /nodes/ai-economy-coachella-valley/ 301
/nodes/sunshinefm-startup-studios /nodes/ai-economy-coachella-valley/ 301

# Second node retirement (2026-08-11), same Pattern C1. ai-startup-activity was
# merged into ai-economy-coachella-valley on 2026-06-03 and left behind as a
# `deprecated: true` stub — a live, indexed, agent-fetchable page whose entire
# body was a notice telling the reader to go somewhere else. The stub was doing
# the redirect's job in prose, which only works on a reader that reads. Five
# briefs linked it, and every one of them ALREADY linked the merge target, so
# the links were removed as duplicates rather than repointed.
/nodes/ai-startup-activity/ /nodes/ai-economy-coachella-valley/ 301
/nodes/ai-startup-activity /nodes/ai-economy-coachella-valley/ 301

# SURFACE retirement (2026-08-11) — /snapshots/ in full, not a single record.
# Pattern C1 was written for one node inside a live collection; this is the
# first time a whole route family goes, so it extends: route template, source
# data, feed, feed generation, sitemap block, llms.txt entries and the
# api-catalog entry all removed in the same commit.
#
# TARGET IS THE MATCHING NODE, PER SLUG — not the homepage. All three snapshot
# slugs already have a live node of the same name, and snapshots.json carried
# `nodeSlug` equal to the slug for all three, so the mapping is the repo's own
# and needed no invention. A snapshot was a scored assessment OF an entity; the
# node is the current record for that same entity, which makes these true
# content redirects rather than a soft landing.
/snapshots/gardens-on-el-paseo/ /nodes/gardens-on-el-paseo/ 301
/snapshots/gardens-on-el-paseo /nodes/gardens-on-el-paseo/ 301
/snapshots/sensei-porcupine-creek/ /nodes/sensei-porcupine-creek/ 301
/snapshots/sensei-porcupine-creek /nodes/sensei-porcupine-creek/ 301
/snapshots/visit-greater-palm-springs/ /nodes/visit-greater-palm-springs/ 301
/snapshots/visit-greater-palm-springs /nodes/visit-greater-palm-springs/ 301

